ut actually less dangerous since W7 dead.  \n3. **Discard F4**: has 4 left, but honors are often discarded early \u2014 prev player discarded J3, J1, others have F1 meld, so F4 might be safe. Keeping F2 for seat+prevailing wind pung possibility (4 left).  \n4. **Discard B3 or B6**: they could fit into chows with B4/B5, so better to keep them for chow potential. B3 with B4? Not chow without B2 or B5. Actually B3 and B4 not consecutive without B2/B5. I have B3, B4\u00d73, B5, B6 \u2014 so B3-B4 not chow, B4-B5 is chow (B4\u00d73, B5\u00d71 \u2192 B4\u2013B5\u2013B6 chow possible with B6). So B3 is somewhat isolated but B3-B4-B5 chow possible if I draw B5 (I have one B5), but to have chow I need another B5 or B2. Might still keep B3 as simple tile.  \n\nBetter to discard **singleton honors or isolated terminals** to move toward a hand with more potential chows/pungs. Among them: W1, W2, T2, F2, F4. T2 is also isolated since T1 dead, T3 only 1 left unseen, chow T2\u2013T3\u2013T4 unlikely (next player already has that meld).  \n\nGiven that my hand has many B4s, keeping B tiles for chows seems good; discard W1 or W2 or F4. F4 is not wind of the round or seat, safest among honors.  \n\n**Final choice**: Discard F4 because:  \n- Not scoring wind (seat/prevailing = F2).  \n- Less useful unless I get a pung (I have 2 F4s, could keep for pair, but I already have W9 pair; having multiple pairs isn\u2019t optimal). Wait \u2014 I have 2 F4s, that\u2019s a pair. If I discard one F4, I lose that pair. But I already have W9 pair, so not needed. But 2 F4s could become pung if I draw 1 more (4 left). Might be worth keeping? Possibly.  \n\nAlternative: Discard W1 or W2. W1 is terminal, less useful for chows given W7 dead; if discard W1, keep W2 maybe with W3 draw for chow. But W2 has only 2 left unseen, harder to get chow.  \n\nBetter to discard the **more dangerous tile**: which is least likely to give others pung?  \nCheck opponents\u2019 discards:  \n- Prev player: played J3, B9, J1, T8, W2, T1, T3, etc. No F4.  \n- Across player: played W6, J2, W2, W7, J2, B6.  \n- Next player: played T1, W5, B2, W6, T1, J1, J2.  \n\nNo one has discarded F4 yet, so maybe someone holds it \u2014 but with 4 left unseen, it\u2019s not extremely dangerous.  \n\nActually, F4 is a wind, often discarded early; since not discarded yet, maybe someone has pair waiting. Could be risky.  \n\nSafer discard: W1 (terminal, 4 left, no one has W1 meld or calls).  \n\n**I\u2019ll choose W1** to minimize risk and keep hand flexible with B chow possibilities.  \n\n**Reason**:  \nDiscarding W1 is relatively safe (4 left unseen, no opponent interest shown), keeps B tiles for potential chows/pungs, retains W9 pair and F4 pair options, and avoids discarding potentially useful B3/B6 for chows. Also preserves F2 for possible seat/prevalent wind pung.  \n\n---\n\n**Answer: Play W1**"}
