7, Play T1, Play T3, Play T3, Play T5, Play W6, Play W9, Play B7, Play J1, Play B5, Play B5.\nThe followings are your valid actions:[Play B4; Play F4; Play T7; Play J1; Play F2]\u3002Your previous invalid answers are: **  \n\nEvaluation: Play J1 is best to keep B4 and F2 for a two-sided wait (B4 for pung, F2 for pair) while maintaining F4 pung for scoring.  \nReason: J1 has only 1 copy left, hardest to pair; B4 and F2 have more live tiles (3 and 4 left respectively), giving better winning probability.  \nAnswer: Play J1.", "reasoning": "", "output": "Evaluation: My hand has two complete melds (B5-B6-B7 Chow, B3-B4-B5 Chow), plus five singles: T7, B4, F2, F4, J1. The goal is to improve towards a winning hand (e.g., completing another meld and a pair). Current hand is far from ready but I need to discard the least useful tile.\n\nValid actions: Play B4, Play F4, Play T7, Play J1, Play F2.  \nConsider tile usefulness and live counts (from unshown tiles):  \n- T7: Only 1 left unshown. Hard to pair or meld, but can form Chow with T5-T6 or T8-T9; very few options.  \n- B4: I already have two B4s in hand? Hand shows [B4: 2] meaning I hold two B4 tiles. B4 has 3 unshown left (total 4 copies, one in a meld of opponent? Actually melds of next player include B1-B2-B3, not B4). So there are 3 B4s available. Keeping two B4s gives chance for a pung (if I draw the third) or use one in a Chow (with B2-B3 or B5-B6).  \n- F2: 4 unshown left, and F2 is both prevailing and seat wind, so a pung of F2 would give extra fan. I have one F2 now, could pair or pung.  \n- F4: I have three F4s (F4: 3 in hand), a complete pung! That's a valuable meld (though not exposed yet). Keeping it is crucial for potential scoring.  \n- J1: Only 1 unshown left, so pairing it is very unlikely.\n\nAmong discards, J1 is the safest: it's an orphan with little chance to pair, and discarding it doesn't break any potential meld in my hand. If I discard B4, I lose a potential pung or chow combination. If I discard 